    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "Small changes here. Since:\r\n- users might want to style both the highlighted and non-highlighted values (like the google instant autocomplete), without having to go for complex CSS selectors (thus, add a class name)\r\n- the widget is called Highlight\r\n\r\nI propose we go for JSX that will output:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!-- query was \"coca\", matches \"co\" -->\r\n<span class=\"ais-Highlight\">\r\n  <span class=\"ais-Highlight__highlighted\">co</span>\r\n  <span class=\"ais-Highlight__nonHighlighted\">ca</span>\r\n</span>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis way we are both consistent and we should answer most use cases of styling/targeting both highlighted and non-highlighted values. Even if the google instant example is a bit different (it's actually suggestions), I believe the use case should still be addressed because doable with Algolia.",
    "commit_id": "8c67ff65113466db56cedefd497df6bb823bd949",
    "created_at": "2016-12-04T20:40:21Z",
    "diff_hunk": "@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@\n+import React from 'react';\n+\n+export default function Highlight({hit, attributeName, highlight}) {\n+  const parsedHighlightedValue = highlight({hit, attributeName});\n+  const reactHighlighted = parsedHighlightedValue.map((v, i) => {\n+    if (!v.isHighlighted) return v.value;\n+    const key = `split-${i}-${v.value}`;\n+    return <em key={key} className=\"ais-highlighted__value\">{v.value}</em>;\n+  });\n+  return <span className=\"ais-highlighted\">{reactHighlighted}</span>;",
